BT 1 0 0 1 0 144.5 Tm 14 TL /F2 9.5 Tf .168627 .176471 .258824 rg (Theorem \(Marginal Validity of Split Conformal Prediction\):) Tj /F1 9.5 Tf  T* (Suppose \(X_1, Y_1\), ..., \(X_n, Y_n\), \(X_{n+1}, Y_{n+1}\) are exchangeable random variables. Let s_i = S\(X_i, Y_i\) be) Tj T* (conformity scores. For alpha in \(0, 1\), let q_hat be the ceil\(\(n+1\)\(1-alpha\)\)/n empirical quantile of s_{1:n}. Then the) Tj T* (prediction set C\(X_{n+1}\) = { y : S\(X_{n+1}, y\) <) Tj (= q_hat } satisfies:) Tj  T*  T* /F2 9.5 Tf (P\( Y_{n+1} in C\(X_{n+1}\) \) >= 1 - alpha) Tj /F1 9.5 Tf   T*  T* /F2 9.5 Tf (Proof Outline:) Tj /F1 9.5 Tf  T* (By exchangeability, the rank of s_{n+1} among s_1, ..., s_n, s_{n+1} is uniformly distributed over {1, 2, ..., n+1}.) Tj T* (Consequently, P\( rank\(s_{n+1}\) <) Tj (= ceil\(\(n+1\)\(1-alpha\)\) \) = ceil\(\(n+1\)\(1-alpha\)\) / \(n+1\) >= 1 - alpha. Since s_{n+1} <) Tj (=) Tj T* (q_hat if and only if its rank is at most ceil\(\(n+1\)\(1-alpha\)\), the finite-sample marginal coverage holds exactly.) Tj T* ET
